eloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)(1+t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-3