{2}{t(1-{t}^{2})}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365383" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}+3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{3} & =3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-{t}^{2}) & =(\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x{t}^{3}+1 \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-3x{t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3x{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3tx}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3tx}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3t}{1-{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{1-{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{1-{t}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-365386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2)-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t=(2t-4){e}^{t/2}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{t/2} & =t{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2}) & =t{e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t/2} & =(2t-4){e}^{t/2}+C \\\\ x & =2t-4+C{e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t+2)-{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)(t+2)-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t+2,\\text{ }g(t)=-{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{t}^{2}+t+2"] ["math" "-{t}^{2}+t-2"] ["math" "-{t}^{2}-t+2"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+t+2"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-t-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{t}^{2}+t+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{4}{t-1} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =4(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =4(t-1) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (4t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-4t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2t"] ["math" "-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t=t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{2}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|}=(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =1-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =1-\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(t)=\\frac{t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "f(t)=\\frac{t-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{t-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{(1-t)(2-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-3+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}-2"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+2t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{2}-2t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =(1-3t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =(1-3t){e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & ={e}^{t}-3(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =4-3t+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+4-3t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=4-3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "555b9439914996c03db0deb6e98ec6b6974c397965c9021bb0e242d47ab5c3ab"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(t)=3-4t"] ["math" "f(t)=4+3t"] ["math" "f(t)=4-3t"] ["math" "f(t)=4-t"] ["math" "f(t)=5-3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "q-337699" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(t)=4-3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "b22253890bdde4bc0a3036018a86ed3124c70c0c94ee5ad27cf224b94901806d"} {"question" "e-21716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n\n- $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n\n- $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21716.json" "file_sha256" "fe1fb7722f47c6bfaf943180cc5d4c548fa1e5a111dde6829e8d819b0685ed8d"} {"question" "e-21716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f(y') = y',$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y').$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21716.json" "file_sha256" "fe1fb7722f47c6bfaf943180cc5d4c548fa1e5a111dde6829e8d819b0685ed8d"} {"question" "e-21773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21773.json" "file_sha256" "b55fe8b9c654c3d1d47f29e88f00335670c1d26d5e09d17c4a03ad39344a2d77"} {"question" "e-21773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =10t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21773.json" "file_sha256" "b55fe8b9c654c3d1d47f29e88f00335670c1d26d5e09d17c4a03ad39344a2d77"} {"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21717.json" "file_sha256" "af22e5b52513c6e08f91dc33de01eeafbeb4a4accf3d64eae61eed3a1bcc62ef"} {"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21717.json" "file_sha256" "af22e5b52513c6e08f91dc33de01eeafbeb4a4accf3d64eae61eed3a1bcc62ef"}] "usage" {"e-21716" [] "e-21717" [] "e-21773" []} "basis" 1923 "retractions" [[17592186228864 :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n1. $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n2. $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n3. $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$"] [17592186228864 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nNote that, in the special case where\n\n$$\nf(y') = y'\n$$\n\nthe equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y$, we get\n$y = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2}$,\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$.\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2$.\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'}$,\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186228884 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$."] [17592186228884 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t)$. We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x$.\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t + 5t^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t) + \\frac{d}{dx}(5t^{2}) \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin t) + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t + 2x\\cos t\\frac{dt}{dx} + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt - 2\\sin t &= (2x\\cos t + 10t)\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{t - 2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t + 10t} &= \\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2x\\cos t + 10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2x\\cos t + 10t \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} - 2x\\cos t &= 10t \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} - \\frac{2\\cos t}{t - 2\\sin t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t - t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$"] [17592186228865 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)$.\n\nIf $y'(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}$"] [17592186228865 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t$, our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{∫P(t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{∫2/(t - 1)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid t - 1 \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (t - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= (t - 1)^{2}(1 + t)/(t(1 - t)) \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n∫\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2})dt &= ∫\\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}dt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= ∫\\frac{1}{t} - tdt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\ln \\mid t\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + C \\\\ x|= (2\\ln \\mid t\\end{vmatrix}- t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= ((2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2}))(t - 1)^{2} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
